The '''Miyoshi Line''' (三好線 ''Miyoshi-sen'') is Line 1 of the [[Okami Metro]], the rapid transit system serving the city of [[Okami]] in [[Okaiken]]. Coloured lavender (#9370DB) on system maps, the line runs approximately 54.5 kilometres from [[Yuyama Chuo]] in the north to [[Okada]] in the south, serving 43 stations along its route. The Miyoshi Line is the primary north–south artery of the Okami Metro, running beneath Miyoshi Boulevard for much of its length and connecting major wards including Yuyama, Sugisawa, Kanda, Aoi, Chiyoda, Chuo, Tama, Tamade, Rokumanji, Minato, Tobe, and Masaki.

Opened in June 1935, the Miyoshi Line holds the distinction of being the first publicly operated subway in Okaiken. Its initial segment between [[Okami Chuo]] and [[Hozumi-Oiwake]] represented a major milestone in the empire's urban infrastructure development, establishing a precedent for underground rapid transit that would later be emulated by other cities across the region. Today, the line is one of the busiest in the Okami Metro network, carrying hundreds of thousands of passengers daily through the heart of the city.

The Miyoshi Line operates using 8-car Type B trains at a maximum speed of 80 km/h, powered by 1,500 V DC overhead catenary on standard 1,435 mm gauge track. The line features 14 interchange stations connecting to other Okami Metro lines as well as commuter rail services, making it a critical backbone of the city's public transportation network. The line was [[privatisation|privatized]] in 2018 as part of broader reforms to the Okami Metro system.

== History ==

The Miyoshi Line's origins date to the early 1930s, when the rapidly growing city of Okami faced severe surface-level traffic congestion along its primary north–south corridor, Miyoshi Boulevard. The Imperial Government approved construction of an underground railway in 1933, and work commenced under the auspices of the newly formed Okami Municipal Transit Bureau. The initial segment, running between [[Okami Chuo]] and [[Hozumi-Oiwake]], opened to the public in June 1935, making the Miyoshi Line the first publicly operated subway in Okaiken. The opening was celebrated with large public ceremonies and was attended by senior imperial officials.

Construction of extensions was halted during the [[Great Adonian War]], as resources and labour were diverted to the war effort. The partially completed tunnels were repurposed as air-raid shelters during the heaviest bombing campaigns, and several stations served as emergency supply depots. Following the war's end, the damaged infrastructure required significant rehabilitation before construction could resume. It was not until the 1960s that the post-war economic recovery provided sufficient funding and political will to continue expanding the line.

In the 1960s and 1970s, the line was extended southward across the strait to [[Tamashima]] island, a feat of engineering that required the construction of a dedicated transit tunnel beneath the waterway. This extension opened in stages between 1964 and 1972 and brought rapid transit service to the densely populated island communities for the first time. The Tamashima extension was widely regarded as one of the most ambitious public works projects of the era and significantly reduced commute times for residents of the southern wards.

The line reached its current extent with a series of extensions to [[Masaki]] and [[Okada]] during the 1990s and 2000s. The final extension to Okada opened in 2005, completing the 54.5 km route. In 2018, as part of a comprehensive reform of the Okami Metro system, the Miyoshi Line was privatized along with the rest of the network, transferring operations from the municipal government to the newly formed Okami Metro Corporation. The privatization also coincided with the introduction of the new 5000 series rolling stock and the refurbishment of legacy 2000 series trains.

== Route description ==

The Miyoshi Line begins at [[Yuyama Chuo]] in the northern ward of Yuyama, heading south beneath Miyoshi Boulevard through the residential districts of Sugisawa and Iwaida. The line then enters the Kanda and Tatara wards, serving the commercial and cultural heart of northern Okami before reaching the Sakura ward at Kawabata-kyo. Continuing south through the Nishi and Aoi wards, the line passes through some of the city's busiest interchange stations, including Kamiya-cho and Hozumi-Oiwake, where connections to the [[Tozai Line (Okami)|Tozai Line]] and other services are available.

In the central section, the line serves the historic Chiyoda ward, including Otemachi and Okami Chuo, the city's primary transportation hub. South of Okami Chuo, the line crosses into the Chuo ward, serving Mugishima and Miyoshi-Nagashima before entering the Tama ward, where the station spacing becomes noticeably tighter due to the dense urban development of this area. The line then continues through Tamade and Rokumanji wards, crossing into the Minato ward along the southern coast.

The final southern section of the line passes through the Tobe ward, serving the industrial and port districts, before reaching the Masaki ward and terminating at [[Okada]]. The southern extension to Masaki and Okada traverses a mix of residential, commercial, and light industrial areas, providing critical transit access to communities that were previously underserved by rapid transit.

