== Rolling stock ==

=== Current ===
* '''5000 series''' — The primary rolling stock of the Miyoshi Line, introduced in 2018 by [[Okami Heavy Industries]]. These 8-car Type B trains feature modern passenger information systems, energy-efficient motors, and improved accessibility features including wheelchair spaces at each end of every car. The 5000 series was designed specifically for the privatized Okami Metro and is shared with other Type B lines in the network.
* '''4000 series''' — The flagship of the Okami Metro fleet, introduced in 2023. The 4000 series features a futuristic exterior design with distinctive hexagonal windows and a sculpted front cab. Interior amenities include full [[Satori Network]] WiFi coverage, USB charging ports at every seat, and real-time passenger information displays. The 4000 series is currently deployed on the busiest services of the Miyoshi Line and is expected to eventually replace the 5000 series as the primary fleet.
* '''2000 series''' — A legacy fleet dating to 1981, these trains were extensively refurbished in 2018 to extend their service life. The refurbishment included new interiors, updated propulsion systems, and improved lighting. However, the 2000 series is being progressively phased out as additional 5000 and 4000 series trains are delivered. The remaining 2000 series trains primarily operate during off-peak hours.

=== Historical ===
* '''1000 series''' — The original rolling stock used when the line first opened in 1935. These trains were retired in the 1960s.
* '''1500 series''' — Introduced in the 1960s for the Tamashima extension. Retired in the 1990s.

== Through-service ==

The Miyoshi Line does not currently operate through-service with any commuter rail lines. However, the line serves as a key interchange point for several other Okami Metro lines, including the [[Tozai Line (Okami)|Tozai Line]] at Kamiya-cho, Hozumi-Oiwake, and several other stations. The 14 interchange stations along the route make the Miyoshi Line a central hub within the Okami Metro network, allowing passengers to transfer to east-west and cross-city services at multiple points.

Plans have been periodically discussed to introduce through-service between the Miyoshi Line and northern commuter rail services via Yuyama Chuo, but as of the latest network revisions, no such service has been implemented.

